{"id":"b93324cd-b75e-4c4f-b4a4-5da7ac382bb7","revision":0,"last_node_id":54,"last_link_id":65,"nodes":[{"id":1,"type":"MarkdownNote","pos":[1210,270],"size":[280,170],"flags":{"pinned":true},"order":0,"mode":0,"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"title":"Models in this Workflow","properties":{},"widgets_values":["### z_image_turbo_int8_convrot:\n[Link to the file](https://huggingface.co/Winnougan/Z-Image-Base-Turbo-INT8-Convrot/tree/main)\n\n### qwen_3_4b_int8_convrot:\n[Link to the file](https://huggingface.co/Winnougan/Z-Image-Base-Turbo-INT8-Convrot/tree/main)\n\n### ae-turbo:\n[Link to the file](https://huggingface.co/Tongyi-MAI/Z-Image-Turbo/tree/main/vae)"],"widgets_values_named":{"text":"### z_image_turbo_int8_convrot:\n[Link to the file](https://huggingface.co/Winnougan/Z-Image-Base-Turbo-INT8-Convrot/tree/main)\n\n### qwen_3_4b_int8_convrot:\n[Link to the file](https://huggingface.co/Winnougan/Z-Image-Base-Turbo-INT8-Convrot/tree/main)\n\n### ae-turbo:\n[Link to the file](https://huggingface.co/Tongyi-MAI/Z-Image-Turbo/tree/main/vae)"},"color":"#2a363b","bgcolor":"#3f5159"},{"id":2,"type":"MarkdownNote","pos":[1210,500],"size":[280,240],"flags":{"pinned":true},"order":1,"mode":0,"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"title":"Note","properties":{},"widgets_values":["## Spatial LoRA - Quick Usage\nPurpose:\nApply up to 3 different LoRAs to specific regions of the image using one single node.\n\n### Basic setup\n1. Load your base model as usual.\n2. Connect the model to Spatial LoRA | Multi LoRA.\n3. Connect width and height from your latent size node (recommended: Empty Latent Size Picker).\n4. Choose up to 3 LoRAs inside the node.\n5. Click Open Spatial Editor.\n6. Create and adjust one region for each LoRA.\n7. Save/apply the regions.\n8. Connect the MODEL output to your sampler.\n\n### Prompting\n- Use your normal prompt as usual.\n- If your LoRA uses a trigger word, include it in the prompt.\n- The plugin applies the LoRA on the model side, not on the CLIP side.\n\n### Notes\n- Each region controls where its LoRA is applied.\n- Feather softens the region edges.\n- Snap helps align boxes.\n- Overlap is allowed, but may increase identity bleed.\n- For a global effect, use a region covering the full image.\n\n### Debug / Preview\n- Use the preview output to inspect masks and region placement.\n- Terminal logs show:\n  - detected backend\n  - resolution\n  - active regions\n  - mask coverage\n  - overlap warnings\n\n### Recommendation\nFor best results:\n- keep regions separated when possible\n- match the editor resolution to the latent resolution\n- use fixed seeds when testing changes"],"widgets_values_named":{"text":"## Spatial LoRA - Quick Usage\nPurpose:\nApply up to 3 different LoRAs to specific regions of the image using one single node.\n\n### Basic setup\n1. Load your base model as usual.\n2. Connect the model to Spatial LoRA | Multi LoRA.\n3. Connect width and height from your latent size node (recommended: Empty Latent Size Picker).\n4. Choose up to 3 LoRAs inside the node.\n5. Click Open Spatial Editor.\n6. Create and adjust one region for each LoRA.\n7. Save/apply the regions.\n8. Connect the MODEL output to your sampler.\n\n### Prompting\n- Use your normal prompt as usual.\n- If your LoRA uses a trigger word, include it in the prompt.\n- The plugin applies the LoRA on the model side, not on the CLIP side.\n\n### Notes\n- Each region controls where its LoRA is applied.\n- Feather softens the region edges.\n- Snap helps align boxes.\n- Overlap is allowed, but may increase identity bleed.\n- For a global effect, use a region covering the full image.\n\n### Debug / Preview\n- Use the preview output to inspect masks and region placement.\n- Terminal logs show:\n  - detected backend\n  - resolution\n  - active regions\n  - mask coverage\n  - overlap warnings\n\n### Recommendation\nFor best results:\n- keep regions separated when possible\n- match the editor resolution to the latent resolution\n- use fixed seeds when testing changes"},"